<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afrikaner Bond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> Bond was a political organisation founded in 1880 to promote the interests of Afrikaners in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, under Jan Hofmeyr, it became the main party of Dutch-speaking farmers and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>'s ministry from 1890 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a> of 1895–1896 broke the alliance. It merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-party">South African Party</a> after Union.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bond was founded as an anti-imperialist party with its origins largely in the Orange Free State. It was formed by the union of the Genootskap van Regte Afrikaners (Society of True Afrikaners) of the Rev S.J. du Toit and the Zuidafrikaansche Boeren Beschermings Vereeniging (South African Farmers' Protection Association) of Jan Hendrik Hofmeyr. A German living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> named Borckenhagen was instrumental in the union and in spreading the party across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and he influenced Reitz, later state secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>.</p><p>Borckenhagen gave its aim as &quot;the States of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> to be federated in one independent republic&quot;, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> branch was less republican and readier to live with an imperial policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>. The Bond claimed to represent all who regarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as their home. These Afrikanders were mostly white farmers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> extraction, but the first Bond was explicitly open to people of all races, and it set out to advance their interests from the Cape to the Limpopo River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Cape Parliament the Bond often held a majority but never governed directly, forming coalitions instead with English-speaking politicians: the ministry of Thomas Scanlen, the administration of Cecil John Rhodes, with which it split after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a>, and the ministry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-x-merriman">John X. Merriman</a>, the last Prime Minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> from 1908 to 1910. In 1882 it secured the recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as an official language of the Cape Colony.</p><p>After the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was formed on 31 May 1910, the Bond ceased to be an independent party. It merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-party">South African Party</a> of the Cape, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/het-volk">Het Volk</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orangia-unie">Orangia Unie</a> of the Orange Free State into a new Union-wide South African Party. It is distinct from the Afrikaner Broederbond, a secret cultural organisation formed in 1918.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afrikaner Bond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cape politics was shaped by the <b>Afrikaner Bond</b> under Jan Hofmeyr and by English-speaking mining interests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
